# .gitignore — identity-threat-detection-lab
#
# .gitignore stops Git from *adding* matching files that are not tracked yet.
# It is NOT a security control on its own: `git add -f` bypasses it, and a file
# that is already committed stays tracked. gitleaks (pre-commit + CI) is the
# second layer that catches secrets this file misses.
#
# Rule syntax used below:
#   name      matches a file or folder with that name anywhere in the repo
#   name/     matches folders only
#   *  **     wildcard inside one folder / across any number of folders
#   !pattern  re-includes something an earlier rule excluded (order matters)
#   #         starts a comment ONLY at the start of a line. A comment after a
#             pattern becomes part of the pattern and silently breaks it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Secrets and credentials — never committed, never pasted into chat
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Real keys and tokens for local runs, plus variants such as .env.local
.env
.env.*
# Placeholders only; this one IS committed (must come after the rules above)
!.env.example
# Private keys and certificate bundles
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12
# Azure publish profiles embed credentials
*.publishsettings
# Copied AWS / Azure CLI credential and token-cache folders
.aws/
.azure/
# A leak report quotes the secret it found
gitleaks-report.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Raw evidence that may hold private data — only redacted copies are committed
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unredacted screenshots (tenant ID, home IP, email visible)
docs/screenshots/raw/
# Raw query exports from the live tenant / AWS account
lab/evidence-raw/
# Velociraptor collections and offline-collector output: real usernames, paths, hashes
dfir/velociraptor/collections/
Collection-*.zip
# Velociraptor server config contains the CA private key; client config is lab-specific
server.config.yaml
client.config.yaml
# Downloaded binaries (Velociraptor, Sysmon, Stratus): fetch and hash-verify, never commit
*.exe
*.msi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Infrastructure-as-Code tool state — can contain secrets and resource IDs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Terraform plugins (large, re-downloadable), state and plans
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
crash.log

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Python leftovers — regenerated on every run
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
# Virtual environments: rebuilt from the lockfile
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Large or local-only runtime data
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Raw public-dataset archives (we commit a small extracted sample + provenance)
data/datasets/_downloads/
# Kusto emulator persisted data, if we mount a folder
kustodata/
# Local LLM model files (several GB)
ollama-data/
# Scratch eval outputs; final scorecards go to docs/results/
evals/runs/
*.log

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Editor, OS and tool-local settings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store
